    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Stop 3: Food chains"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "markdown": "Every food chain starts with a **producer**, a plant that makes its own food using sunlight. Animals that eat plants are **herbivores**; animals that eat other animals are **carnivores**. Grass, then rabbit, then fox is a food chain."
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "multiple_choice",
        "prompt": "In the chain grass, rabbit, fox, which one is the producer?",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "text": "Grass"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "text": "Rabbit"
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "text": "Fox"
          }
        ],
        "answer": [
          "a"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "activity",
      "title": "My food chain",
      "materials": [
        "paper",
        "coloured pencils"
      ],
      "instructions": [
        "Draw a food chain with at least 4 living things.",
        "Label the producer, the herbivore and the carnivore.",
        "Add arrows that point to what gets the energy."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Stop 4: Forces and magnets"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "markdown": "Forces can speed things up, slow them down or change their shape. **Friction** slows things that rub together. **Gravity** pulls things toward the Earth. A **magnet** pulls some metals, like iron, but not wood or plastic."
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "multiple_choice",
        "prompt": "Which force pulls objects down to the ground?",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "text": "Gravity"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "text": "Friction"
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "text": "Magnetism"
          }
        ],
        "answer": [
          "a"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Stop 5: Light, sound and circuits"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "markdown": "Light travels in straight lines, so objects block it and make **shadows**. Sound is made by **vibrations**. An electric **circuit** needs a complete loop with a battery, wires and a bulb for the bulb to light."
    },
    {
      "type": "check",
      "question": {
        "type": "true_false",
        "prompt": "A bulb will light if the circuit has a gap in it.",
        "answer": [
          false
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "reveal",
      "prompt": "Why is your shadow longer in the evening than at noon?",
      "answer_markdown": "The Sun is low in the sky in the evening, so light hits you at a shallow angle and the shadow stretches out."
    },
    {
      "type": "brain_game",
      "game": "remember_order",
      "title": "Water cycle memory",
      "goal": "Watch the order, then copy it.",
      "emojis": [
        "🌞",
        "💧",
        "☁️",
        "🌧️"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "summary",
      "points": [
        "Solids, liquids and gases behave differently and can change state.",
        "Water moves round in a cycle.",
        "Food chains start with plants that make their own food.",
        "Forces push or pull and gravity pulls things down.",
        "A circuit must be a complete loop."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "style": "tip",
      "markdown": "Ready? Start the quiz. It has 12 questions and about 15 minutes. A guess is better than a blank."
    },
    {
      "type": "glossary",
      "terms": [
        {
          "term": "evaporate",
          "meaning": "Liquid water turns into an invisible gas called water vapour."
        },
        {
          "term": "condense",
          "meaning": "A gas cools down and turns back into tiny drops of liquid."
        },
        {
          "term": "producer",
          "meaning": "A plant that makes its own food using sunlight."
        },
        {
          "term": "carnivore",
          "meaning": "An animal that eats other animals."
        },
        {
          "term": "herbivore",
          "meaning": "An animal that eats only plants."
        },
        {
          "term": "friction",
          "meaning": "A force that slows things down when they rub together."
        },
        {
          "term": "gravity",
          "meaning": "The force that pulls things toward the ground."
        },
        {
          "term": "circuit",
          "meaning": "A complete loop that electricity can flow around."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
